00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
well i do welcome this announcement  today from the home secretary i've been  speaking to on a daily basis over the  last few days working out what we can do  to restore the shuttle  people across the country uh have and  the announcement today means uh we'll be  looking to firstly what happened in  relation to this serving officer  who was able to abuse his power  to abduct sarah average  and unfortunately rape and murder her  uh see if any opportunities that were  missed  to make sure those are addressed an  accident taken  and then the second part of the inquiry  is to look at wider issues raised  from uh these shoes that we're hearing  about on a daily basis now  of misogyny sexism racism and homophobia  and i make this point the vast vast vast  majority of police officers are  incredibly brave men and women who run  towards danger asking us to go to safety  but we can't escape the fact  that the confidence the british public  have in the police has been shattered  uh by the awful uh sarah the situation  will happen to sarah her murder  the home secretary referred to said so  the public will have the right to know  what systematic failures enabled his  continued employment what's wrong with  the met  well i think it's a problem not just in  that police service but we look at what  happens across police and many of us  felt shocked betrayed in a sense of  shame when we heard the details in  relation to not just uh sarah's  abduction  uh uh rape and murder but also the fact  that this police officer apparently had  had allegations made against him at kent  police  in the civil nuclear constabulary he  appears to have passed the vetting  procedures he appears to in a whatsapp  group  have been sharing frankly speaking  offensive uh homophobic racist sexist  messages with other servant police  officers and so there are all sorts of  issues that need urgent answers to  that's why  i support this inquiry in the way the  home sector is set up because we want  urgent answers and urgent action how  does a veteran work in relation to  people joining the police service people  transferring between police services how  to ensure  there isn't a culture in the police  service whereby somebody who's worried  about the behavior of others  inappropriate behavior inappropriate  language involved confident reporting  and kicking out of the police service  those who aren't fit to be police  officers well the usual way that  organizations do go about this is  changing the way that those  organizations are led and and we've just  seen christopher dick being reappointed  you've described her as experienced and  a strong leader and have welcomed her  reappointment do you think the person  that was in charge while all of these  things  went wrong including the vetting and  we're not even going to talk about all  the other things that that she's been  involved in that there's been criticism  of  do you not think that it it is time for  her to step down one of the things  that's quite clear uh from all the uh  events i'm holding  uh is that replacing one person is not  going to address the serious issue we've  got in relation to the concerns being  raised not just in the police service  the hmi did a report very recently and  she used the word epidemic when  describing the issue of violence against  women and girls across our society it's  a reality  i'm afraid that between international  women's day last year  and this year 118 women were killed at  the hands of uh men and so we've got to  make sure of course  we get the police's house in order i  think as a society we've got to address  the issue of misogyny i think it should  be a hate crime i think the harassment  of women in a public place should be  criminal offense i think we're going to  start in primary school but i think  you're right we've got to make sure our  police services across the country  have the trust of confidence which i  think has been shattered  by the events of the last couple of  weeks but the conf there's no confidence  within the force as well though i spoke  to a female former officer just last  week um and she said on on the sarah  everard sentencing and she said that  that she would was scared to stand up to  males who'd misbehaved around her within  the force during the current leadership  because she didn't know if then when she  went out on a job if she got into  trouble they would back her up now i  mean that is no way to run an  organization is it if women within that  organization don't feel safe surely the  responsibility for that goes all the way  to the top  well i can't speak about the individual  ex-officer what i can say is part two  of the inquiry announced today from the  home secretary we'll look at just those  sorts of uh issue  are there procedures in place where  women in particular feel safe coming  forward with a grievance is the counter  corruption work fit for purpose is the  whistleblowing process is fit for  purpose are we creating an environment  in a culture where we want to join the  police service and not just stay in a  police service but we can make  progression all those are some of the  issues we have okay in part two but also  the hmi her majesty inspector the  independent office for police conduct  also have a role to play as well as this  independent chair who homosexual will be  appointed very shortly  okay so say in in a few months time when  we hope that this inquiry will end and  it won't just drag on forever  save in a few months time we discover  that the answer to those questions is no  that women don't feel safe within the  police force uh that they don't feel  they're able to to report upwards when  they see an appropriate behavior what  happens then we don't make changes we've  got to make sure no stone is left  unturned  in relation to making uh the police a  service that is free from racism but how  do you change it that that's my my  question you know it's  yes we need to find out exactly what's  going on but but how how does it change  how do you change something as huge as  the police force and the mentality of  the people working within it well  firstly we've got to ascertain the facts  not just in relation to this police  officer and sarah's awful murder but in  relation to the missed opportunities  then we've got to make sure all those  are addressed in relation to urgent  implementation of the recommendations  made by independent chair  then in relation to the wider practices  in the police service we've got to see  what the inquiry uncovers in relation to  practices across the country you and i  have been reading on a daily basis and  hearing on a daily basis from offices  across the country but also women let  down uh by the criminal justice system  women not feeling confident to report  crimes a huge issue of under-reporting  and those that do report uh crimes for  example rape and other serious offenses  the conviction rates are so so uh small  and that's why it's really important  this is a whole government approach we  can't just leave this all of the door  the police the issue of misogyny didn't  start with the police i won't end with  the police it's going to be not helped  by the police though is it clearly  listen nobody can talk about the awful  uh murder of sarah  and not you know agree that public  confidence has been uh shattered it is  simply indefensible  not just the actions of that police  officer but the apparent missed  opportunities uh it's not acceptable  indeed sadiq khan always got to talk to  you thank you for joining us on sky

